            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Background: Common Challenges in Multi-Account Management</h2>
<p>As advertising teams expand their overseas operations, they often face this scenario: 5 Google accounts, 8 Meta accounts, and a dozen TikTok accounts scattered across different platforms, each corresponding to different product lines, markets, or campaign stages. With limited operations staff, everyone needs to monitor spending, budgets, and performance across all accounts simultaneously.</p>
<p>The results are often:</p>
<ul>
<li>Key accounts don't receive priority attention, and potential accounts are overlooked</li>
<li>Account issues are discovered too late, missing optimization windows</li>
<li>New team members struggle to get started, and account assets are difficult to hand over</li>
</ul>
<p>The goal of this SOP is to enable any operations colleague to clarify management priorities within 30 minutes of receiving the account list and execute daily operations on a fixed schedule.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Establish Account Classification Dimensions</h2>
<p>Before tiering, label each account by business attributes. It is recommended to classify across three dimensions:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Classification</th>
<th>Common Types</th>
<th>Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Product Line</strong></td>
<td>Main product, test product, long-tail product</td>
<td>Different products correspond to different ROI targets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Market Region</strong></td>
<td>Mature market, emerging market, test market</td>
<td>Market stage affects budget allocation strategy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Account Source</strong></td>
<td>Initial account, additional account, legacy account</td>
<td>Source affects troubleshooting approach</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Operation Method</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li>Export all account lists, including Account ID, name, and creation date</li>
<li>Label each account with tags from the three dimensions above</li>
<li>Record tags uniformly in shared documents or project management tools (e.g., Feishu Spreadsheet, Jira)</li>
<li>When creating new accounts, complete classification labels synchronously</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: Once classification labels are established, keep them stable. If product lines adjust, use version tracking rather than frequent label modifications to maintain history.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Define Account Priority Tiers</h2>
<p>Based on classification results, prioritize accounts by business importance and spending scale. A three-tier system (A/B/C) is recommended:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Tier</th>
<th>Criteria</th>
<th>Daily Spending Reference</th>
<th>Management Frequency</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>A (Core Accounts)</strong></td>
<td>Main product + mature market, or Top 20% by spending</td>
<td>≥$1000/day</td>
<td>Daily check</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>B (Growth Accounts)</strong></td>
<td>Products in testing phase, markets with incremental budget</td>
<td>$200-1000/day</td>
<td>Every two days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>C (Exploration Accounts)</strong></td>
<td>Long-tail products, new market exploration</td>
<td>&lt;$200/day</td>
<td>Weekly check</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Dynamic Adjustment Rules</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Conduct monthly review, re-tier based on past 30-day spending data</li>
<li>If a C-tier account grows 3x in single-week spending, temporarily upgrade to B-tier management</li>
<li>For accounts with sustained daily spending below $50 and persistently low ROI, consider pausing or consolidating</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Develop Tiered Management Operation Checklists</h2>
<h3>3.1 A-Tier Account Daily SOP (Recommended time: 9:00-9:30 AM)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Review yesterday's total spending, compare with budget, confirm deviation is within ±15%</li>
<li>[ ] Check CTR, CPC, and ROAS for anomalies across campaigns (compare with 7-day average)</li>
<li>[ ] Confirm sufficient budget; initiate top-up alert when balance falls below 3x daily average spending</li>
<li>[ ] Record key daily data to the report</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.2 B-Tier Account Bi-Weekly SOP (Recommended time: Tuesday and Thursday mornings)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Verify cumulative spending over the past two days, confirm no overspending</li>
<li>[ ] Spot-check 2-3 campaigns, focus on newly enabled ad groups</li>
<li>[ ] Check ad review status, confirm no unexpected disapprovals</li>
<li>[ ] Update campaign logs, record optimization actions</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.3 C-Tier Account Weekly SOP (Recommended time: Monday morning)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Summarize last week's spending and performance data, compare with monthly targets</li>
<li>[ ] Filter Top 3 spending campaigns, evaluate if budget upgrade is warranted</li>
<li>[ ] Clear ad groups with no spending for 2 consecutive weeks</li>
<li>[ ] Complete weekly account health score in the report</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Configure Account Permissions and Collaboration Rules</h2>
<p>In multi-account scenarios, permission confusion is a common risk point. The following principles are recommended:</p>
<p><strong>Permission Tiering</strong>:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Role</th>
<th>Operational Scope</th>
<th>Typical Personnel</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><strong>Administrator</strong></td>
<td>All accounts, including top-ups and payment method modifications</td>
<td>Team lead</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Primary Operator</strong></td>
<td>Ad operations and reporting for A/B-tier accounts</td>
<td>Senior operations staff</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Secondary Operator</strong></td>
<td>Daily operations and reporting for C-tier accounts</td>
<td>Junior operations staff</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><strong>Finance Viewer</strong></td>
<td>Top-up records and invoice downloads only</td>
<td>Finance staff</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Key Constraints</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Top-up operations must be performed by administrators or designated finance staff; operations staff do not hold top-up permissions</li>
<li>High-risk operations (deleting accounts, modifying time zones) require dual confirmation</li>
<li>All account credentials are managed uniformly in password managers to avoid direct login with personal accounts</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Establish Exception Alert Mechanisms</h2>
<p>Whether account problems can be detected quickly often determines the extent of losses. The following alert scenarios are recommended:</p>
<p><strong>Required Alert Items</strong>:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Balance Alert</strong>: Account balance &lt; 1.5x of 3-day average daily spending</li>
<li><strong>Overspending Alert</strong>: Single account daily spending &gt; 1.2x daily budget</li>
<li><strong>Payment Failure Alert</strong>: Ad delivery interrupted with payment-related issues</li>
<li><strong>Review Anomaly Alert</strong>: Active ad count drops suddenly by more than 30%</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Implementation Advice</strong>: Automated notifications can be achieved through the Yiyu account management platform's balance alert feature, or configure bot notifications in Feishu/DingTalk.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Conclusion: Core Logic of Account Tiering Management</h2>
<p>Account tiering is not a one-time task but a sustained management habit. The core lies in three points:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Unified Classification</strong>: The entire team uses a single classification standard to avoid fragmentation</li>
<li><strong>Dynamic Tiering</strong>: Adjust regularly based on performance data, directing resources toward efficient accounts</li>
<li><strong>Standardized Operations</strong>: Print SOPs and post them at workstations so new team members can get started quickly</li>
</ul>
<p>After fully implementing this SOP, teams can maintain clear organization even when managing 20+ accounts, freeing energy for actual optimization work.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Recommended Tools</strong>: Yiyu Media supports unified balance monitoring across multiple accounts, top-up record reconciliation, and balance alert notifications, effectively supporting the execution of this SOP.</p>
